  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p17-q16-definition",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Egyptian fraction",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is the sum of reciprocals of distinct natural numbers. For example, ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is an Egyptian fraction with three terms. However, ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\frac13+\\frac13",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is not an Egyptian fraction, since ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\frac13",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is repeated.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Can 1 be written as an Egyptian fraction with two terms?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No.\n\nThe largest possible sum below 1 is $\\frac12+\\frac13=\\frac56<1$.",
          "worked": "A term $\\frac11$ would make the sum greater than 1. With distinct denominators greater than 1, the largest possible sum is\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac12+\\frac13&=\\frac36+\\frac26\\\\\\frac12+\\frac13&=\\frac56<1.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore no two-term representation exists.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Write 1 as an Egyptian fraction with three terms. Is your answer unique?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac16$. Unique, apart from term order.\n\nThe first two denominators are forced to be 2 and 3.",
          "worked": "Order the distinct denominators as $a<b<c$. None can be 1. If $a\\ge3$, the sum is at most $\\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{47}{60}<1$, so $a=2$. If $b\\ge4$, the remaining sum is at most $\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac9{20}<\\frac12$, so $b=3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1-\\frac12-\\frac13&=\\frac66-\\frac36-\\frac26\\\\1-\\frac12-\\frac13&=\\frac16.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $c=6$, giving the unique representation $1=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac16$, apart from term order.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find all the ways in which 1 can be written as an Egyptian fraction with four terms.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$\\begin{aligned}1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac17+\\frac1{42}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac18+\\frac1{24}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac19+\\frac1{18}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac1{10}+\\frac1{15}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15+\\frac1{20}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac16+\\frac1{12}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThese are all six representations, apart from term order.",
          "worked": "Write the denominators in increasing order as $a<b<c<d$. A denominator of 1 is impossible. If $a\\ge3$, the sum is at most $\\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15+\\frac16=\\frac{19}{20}<1$, so $a=2$. If $b\\ge6$, the remaining sum is at most $\\frac16+\\frac17+\\frac18=\\frac{73}{168}<\\frac12$. If $b=5$, then $\\frac1c+\\frac1d=\\frac3{10}$. For $c=6$, this forces $\\frac1d=\\frac2{15}$, so $d=\\frac{15}{2}$ is not a natural number. For $c\\ge7$, the sum is at most $\\frac17+\\frac18=\\frac{15}{56}<\\frac3{10}$. Hence $b=3$ or $b=4$.\n\nFor $b=3$, the remaining sum is $\\frac16$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1c+\\frac1d&=\\frac16\\\\\\frac{c+d}{cd}&=\\frac16\\\\cd-6c-6d&=0\\\\(c-6)(d-6)&=36.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $6<c<d$, the unequal positive factor pairs of 36 give $(c,d)=(7,42),(8,24),(9,18),(10,15)$.\n\nFor $b=4$, the remaining sum is $\\frac14$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1c+\\frac1d&=\\frac14\\\\\\frac{c+d}{cd}&=\\frac14\\\\cd-4c-4d&=0\\\\(c-4)(d-4)&=16.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $4<c<d$, the unequal positive factor pairs of 16 give $(c,d)=(5,20),(6,12)$.\n\nTherefore all representations are\n$$\\begin{aligned}1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac17+\\frac1{42}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac18+\\frac1{24}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac19+\\frac1{18}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac1{10}+\\frac1{15}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15+\\frac1{20}\\\\1&=\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac16+\\frac1{12}.\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 180
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Suppose $n$ is a natural number with $n>1$. Prove that $\\frac1n$ can always be written as an Egyptian fraction with two terms. Hint: evaluate $\\frac1{n+1}+\\frac1{n(n+1)}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac1n=\\frac1{n+1}+\\frac1{n(n+1)}$.\n\nFor natural $n>1$, the denominators are distinct natural numbers.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{n+1}+\\frac1{n(n+1)}&=\\frac{n}{n(n+1)}+\\frac1{n(n+1)}\\\\\\frac1{n+1}+\\frac1{n(n+1)}&=\\frac{n+1}{n(n+1)}\\\\\\frac1{n+1}+\\frac1{n(n+1)}&=\\frac1n.\\end{align*}$$\nFor natural $n>1$, $n+1<n(n+1)$, so both denominators are natural and distinct. This is an Egyptian fraction with two terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Hence, write $\\frac1{10}$ as an Egyptian fraction with two terms.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac1{10}=\\frac1{11}+\\frac1{110}$.",
          "worked": "Using part (d) with $n=10$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{10}&=\\frac1{10+1}+\\frac1{10(10+1)}\\\\\\frac1{10}&=\\frac1{11}+\\frac1{110}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Suppose $n$ and $k$ are natural numbers with $n>1$ and $k\\ge1$. Prove that $\\frac1n$ can always be written as an Egyptian fraction with exactly $k$ terms.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Start with $\\frac1n$ and repeatedly split the term with the largest denominator using part (d).\n\nEach split preserves the sum and distinctness, and increases the number of terms by 1.",
          "worked": "For $k=1$, $\\frac1n$ already has one term. Suppose a representation has distinct denominators, with largest denominator $m$. By part (d),\n$$\\frac1m=\\frac1{m+1}+\\frac1{m(m+1)}.$$\nReplace only $\\frac1m$ by these two terms. Since $m>1$,\n$$m<m+1<m(m+1).$$\nBoth new denominators exceed every old denominator and are distinct from each other. The sum is unchanged and the number of terms increases by exactly 1. Repeating this replacement $k-1$ times gives exactly $k$ terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      }
    ]
  },
